+// link-openmw.sh) while the page fetches the bytes, instead of the page's thread spinning on shared memory (Orthros'
+// src/vfs/data-client.js fetchSync, wantWait): the page answers input, draws its own indicator and presents what the
+// engine drew so far. Linked after the system library (--js-library): fd_read and fd_sync replace libwasi's.
+//
+// Where it may suspend: only under the engine's promising exports — omw_pump_frame (a load's frame) and main() (the
+// boot) —, which say so (orthros_jspi_enter / orthros_jspi_leave, engine.cpp, main.cpp), one suspension at a time.
+// A frame of play is the plain export (omw_pump_frame_plain: the page's own stack, nothing suspends — as before r18);
+// the page pumps the promising one only for a load: one put off to the next frame by the engine (orthros_jspi_defer: a
+// save's load, a new game, the debug COE asked in a plain frame), or the frame of a loading screen still on. A load
+// that starts in a frame of play by itself — a door, an exterior crossing — runs synchronously there, as r16's. Why: a
+// frame in play gained nothing from suspending (docs/openmw.md), and a JSPI stack is exposed to Chromium's native stack
+// walker (base::debug::TraceStackFramePointers, its heap profiler's on a sampled allocation): from that stack's first
+// frame it scans 8 KB further for a frame pointer, past the stack's end — the renderer's crash (2026-10-29/30, the
+// kernel's log: the same instruction each time; at every run with the heap profiler forced on every renderer, never
+// with r16). The promising exports' stacks are made deep first (engine.cpp orthros_jspi_deep: 1,024 plain wasm frames
+// at their base), so that walker, which stops at 255 frames, never reaches the end. An import that suspends (WebAssembly.Suspending) throws SuspendError when called outside
